{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_012UgCbSPHEzDDf4CRcNo4Uk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, there's an issue with the exile-instead replacement. Looking at the zones.py code:\n\n```python\nif replacement_event is not None and leaving_battlefield:\n    replacement_event = game.replacement_manager.apply(game, replacement_event)\n    if getattr(replacement_event, \"prevented\", False):\n        return\n    destination_str = getattr(replacement_event, \"destination\", \"graveyard\")\n    dest_zone = _DESTINATION_ZONE_MAP.get(destination_str, to_zone)\n```\n\nReplacement effects for zone moves are only applied when `leaving_battlefield` is True (from_zone == Zone.BATTLEFIELD). \n\nBut when an instant or sorcery resolves and moves from the stack to the graveyard, that's not leaving the battlefield—it's leaving the stack. So the replacement effect won't trigger in `move_to_zone` at all. The spell resolution logic moves the card from stack to graveyard, but the replacement manager never gets a chance to intercept it.\n\nI'm realizing the plan to use a replacement effect for this won't work as written. The `_resolve_spell` function doesn't pass a `replacement_event` when moving spells to the graveyard, so the replacement mechanism never gets consulted. I need to either modify the spell resolution path to check replacement effects, or find a different approach entirely—though changing the core resolution logic could break existing tests. The replacement event system is designed for battlefield exits, so it won't apply here even if I pass it. I need to either extend `move_to_zone` to handle replacements for graveyard moves from the stack, or bypass the replacement system entirely and hook into the spell's resolution to redirect the zone move to exile instead.\n\nThe cleanest approach is to modify `_resolve_spell` to create a replacement event specifically for when instants and sorceries move from the stack to the graveyard, then register that through the replacement manager.\n\nWriting the modified resolver... Implementing the replacement effect... The issue is that the base class property returns None, so the condition checking won't work. I'm adding a custom attribute directly to the event object as a workaround—storing the card there and then checking it in the condition function using getattr with a fallback. It's not the cleanest approach, but it's localized and minimal.\n\nActually, I'm realizing I could simplify this further by wrapping the StackObject's on_resolve method directly instead of relying on the replacement manager. Once the spell is cast free and lands on the stack, I can intercept its resolution to redirect it to exile instead of the graveyard. Wrapping the original on_resolve callback so that after it executes, I check if the card ended up in the graveyard and move it to exile instead.\n\nThis approach is self-contained and doesn't require touching the engine or registering replacement effects. For the countered case, that's more complex since the counter function directly puts the spell in the graveyard, so I'll focus on handling resolution for now and document the limitation. I'll go with the wrapping approach for resolution only and document the limitation where countered spells still go to the graveyard. This keeps the change localized to the card itself. Let me update the implementation now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1556,"cache_read_input_tokens":146263,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1556},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ecc45bba-5220-4378-9482-c0a0c3fff5e1","uuid":"2705f9c4-f7b8-4322-aacb-7c18308fc09c","request_id":"req_011Cbw3QdDfe6z7BBSmRKtTK"