{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_016Vkm2U8hgzr9u8xJiwkPvs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Looking at the ETB trigger code, I see the issue:\n\nIn `zones.py` lines 301-316:\n```python\nif entering_battlefield:\n    # Fire the ENTERS_BATTLEFIELD event *before* registering the card's\n    # own triggers so that a permanent's ETB trigger does not\n    # retroactively trigger off its own entry.  Other already-registered\n    # triggers that watch for ENTERS_BATTLEFIELD will still fire.\n    game.trigger_manager.fire_event(\n        game,\n        EntersBattlefieldTriggeredEvent(\n            permanent=card,\n            controller=controller if controller is not None else owner,\n        ),\n    )\n    if hasattr(card, \"register_triggers\"):\n        card.register_triggers(game)\n```\n\nSo the engine fires `EntersBattlefieldTriggeredEvent` before registering the card's own triggers. This means the card's ETB trigger gets registered after the event fires, so it never triggers at all. When I call `move_to_zone(game, card, Zone.HAND, Zone.BATTLEFIELD)`, the engine fires the event first, then registers the card's triggers—which is backwards.\n\nThe comment claims \"a permanent's ETB trigger does not retroactively trigger off its own entry,\" but that's not how Magic actually works. The permanent enters, state-based actions check, and then triggered abilities fire. The real issue here is that other already-registered triggers watching for entries will fire, but the permanent's own trigger won't because it hasn't been registered yet. I need to either pre-register the trigger before the card enters, or fire the ETB effect directly through a different hook.\n\nLet me look at how the test helper handles entering the battlefield...\n\nExamining the helper function, I see it moves the card to the battlefield and then resolves the stack, but the ETB event fires before the trigger gets registered. The real issue is that triggers need to be set up before the card actually enters, not after. Let me check how other FDN cards with ETB triggers handle this situation. The comment's actually describing the engine's current behavior, which contradicts how MTG actually works—ETB triggers should fire on the permanent's own entry. I'm wondering if FDN cards use a different mechanism like an `on_enter` method to handle their ETB effects instead.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21402,"cache_read_input_tokens":17121,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21402},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"messages_changed","cache_missed_input_tokens":18223}},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ecc45bba-5220-4378-9482-c0a0c3fff5e1","uuid":"0ecac651-da0f-4276-b1ff-d1874b7ed79b","request_id":"req_011Cbw5r4fnucHTaGr4PADAN"}
