{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Qo8p2YZbUsZbiMkmxNP6ML","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, I see now. Looking at `casting.py` line 427-432:\n```python\ncard.on_resolve(game)\n\nif card.card_types & _PERMANENT_TYPES:\n    # Move from stack to battlefield via move_to_zone, which handles\n    # trigger/replacement-effect registration and ENTERS_BATTLEFIELD event.\n    move_to_zone(game, card, Zone.STACK, Zone.BATTLEFIELD)\n```\n\nSo `on_resolve` is called FIRST, then `move_to_zone(STACK → BATTLEFIELD)`. This is the casting pipeline for creatures.\nMy test helper is putting the card in hand, moving it directly to the battlefield, then resolving the stack. But that's not matching the actual casting flow—the real pipeline puts it on the stack first, calls `on_resolve`, then moves it to the battlefield which triggers the ETB event and registers any resulting triggers.\n\nI'm realizing there's something off with the order here. The ETB event fires before `register_triggers` is called, so any trigger registered after the event fires wouldn't catch it. I need to look at the actual `move_to_zone` implementation to understand if there's a second ETB event being fired for the registered triggers, or if I'm misunderstanding the flow entirely.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1386,"cache_read_input_tokens":47680,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1386},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ecc45bba-5220-4378-9482-c0a0c3fff5e1","uuid":"b55b2eae-3c55-4d5c-87a9-155ff304fc7f","request_id":"req_011Cbw5xEnR6BB78v1GwFjrY"}
