{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01D2BNwjXVFkBwS8MjQSeYig","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the full planeswalker picture. The `LoyaltyAbilityInstance` doesn't have a targeting mechanism built in. For Kaito's +1, it uses `getattr(pw, '_resolve_target', None)` which would have been set externally. For the -1 ability that discards from \"any number of target players\", targeting doesn't use the standard mechanism.\n\nFor sos_97 (Ral Zarek):\n1. **+1: Surveil 2**: Look at top 2 cards, ask about each\n2. **-1: Any number of target players each discard a card**: Choose players, each discards\n3. **-2: Return target creature card with MV ≤ 3 from graveyard to battlefield**: Pick a creature from the graveyard\n4. **-7: Flip five coins, target opponent skips X turns**: Randomize coin flips based on the result\n\nFor the -1 ability, I'll prompt the controller to select which players should discard, then have each chosen player discard a card.\n\nImplementing multiple target selection... Writing the ability logic... I could test by setting a fixed seed to control the random outcomes, or I could replace the coin flip function entirely using a module-level variable that tests can override. The second approach is cleaner — I'll define a `_coin_flip_fn` at the module level that defaults to using `random.random()`, then in tests I can swap it out with a lambda that always returns True or False to control the behavior.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement the sos_97 card and update the KEY_DECISIONS.md file to document this approach.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":595,"cache_read_input_tokens":108492,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":595},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_managem